WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Two decades have passed, but like so many people,&nbsp;
I can still clearly remember where I was when 9/11&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.680
happened. I was on shift, running a planning&nbsp;
shift for DW when four civilian air planes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:18.640
were hijacked and turned into weapons. These&nbsp;
pictures, the collapsing Twin Towers, the fear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.680
the screams, the people, the dust - all of&nbsp;
that unfolding in real time on television.&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:33.520
Very soon attention turned to one country,&nbsp;
Afghanistan. Why? Because of al Qaeda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.800
The terrorist network had established&nbsp;
its main base in Afghanistan and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:45.600
identified as the perpetrator of 9/11.
So to hunt down al-Qaida, the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.840
and its allies invaded
Afghanistan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:54.800
which back then was ruled by the Taliban regime.
Now, 20 years later, the international forces have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.120
withdrawn - defeated. And the Taliban are back&nbsp;
in power. And this, of course, poses one burning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:07.120
question: Will Afghanistan once again become a&nbsp;
safe haven for terrorist groups like al-Qaida?&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.840
We've been trying to drain al-Qaida&nbsp;
support for the past 20 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.880
But every time we use a drone that&nbsp;
kills civilians, we're contributing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.920
sympathy and support for al-Qaida.
Al-Qaida definitely never disappeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:32.240
from the global picture. I'm very convinced&nbsp;
that once they have established a stronghold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.600
in Afghanistan with the support of Taliban, they&nbsp;
will again try to reach out on the global scale.&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.960
I'm DW’s Sandra Petersmann and I’ve been&nbsp;
reporting on Afghanistan for the past two decades.&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:51.520
Afghanistan in 2001 had already suffered two&nbsp;
decades of consecutive war. First, there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:57.760
the Soviet occupation of the 1980s. Then there&nbsp;
was the Civil War, very bloody, of the 1990s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:04.080
And it was precisely this civil war that brought&nbsp;
the Taliban to power and also led to al-Qaida&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.640
establishing its main base in Afghanistan.
Al-Qaida always had the goal to aim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:16.080
geopolitically, so they wanted to establish&nbsp;
a worldwide caliphate. Whereas the Taliban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.280
was a regionally focused group and&nbsp;
concentrated on Afghanistan especially.&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.960
Both of the organizations being jihadist&nbsp;
groups, but with a different focus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:32.000
they very early agreed that they could reach&nbsp;
their aims more successfully when they cooperated.&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.960
I think it was a marriage of convenience. Al-Qaida&nbsp;
was looking for a safe haven or sanctuary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.880
The Taliban were looking for military assistance&nbsp;
and support. And al-Qaida had that in droves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:51.840
It had the expertise. It had battle hardened&nbsp;
fighters. It had bomb makers and commanders.&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:59.440
From all that we know, al-Qaida had attacked&nbsp;
Western entities from Afghanistan before. In 1998&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:06.240
two US embassies in Africa were attacked, the one&nbsp;
in Kenya and the one in Tanzania. And we did see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:13.040
retaliatory strikes after that from the US hitting&nbsp;
targets in Afghanistan. But then of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:19.440
9/11 was an entirely different level.
9/11 put al-Qaida on a different scale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:24.960
In fact, it catapulted them into a different&nbsp;
stratosphere in how we assess terrorist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:31.840
groups. This was a highly complex, well planned,&nbsp;
spectacular terrorist attack that shook the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.480
States to its core. And in my mind, fundamentally&nbsp;
change the way we look at global security.&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:44.080
The US government demanded that the Taliban&nbsp;
hand over al-Qaida's leadership and the Taliban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:53.200
refused. And then on October 7, 2001, barely a&nbsp;
month after the 9/11 attacks, the first US bombs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:04:00.400
dropped on Afghanistan and foot soldiers followed.
What happened with the beginning of the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:06.320
invasion in Afghanistan was a very quick&nbsp;
regime change. The Taliban regime was toppled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.440
but went into hiding, and with it into&nbsp;
hiding went al-Qaida's leadership.&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:19.280
Bin Laden fled. Many of his top lieutenants fled,&nbsp;
went into Pakistan where they received sanctuary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:24.400
And throughout 2001, until bin&nbsp;
Laden's death ten years later in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.320
al-Qaida continued to launch attacks. You had&nbsp;
involvement in the Madrid train attacks in 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.760
the following year, the 7/7 bombings in London.
In the past 20 years al-Qaida hasn't stopped with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.960
terrorist attacks. They were just on a smaller&nbsp;
scale and not emanating from Afghanistan. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:47.520
emanated from different countries as al-Qaida&nbsp;
has diversified, metastasized and become even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.720
more a network of independent elements.
It was able to decentralize. And it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:59.280
able to develop affiliates and branches worldwide&nbsp;
from the Levant to affiliates all over the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.310
frankly. From North Africa down to Southeast.
Al Qaeda's training camps in Afghanistan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:02.880
were destroyed, but that does not mean that&nbsp;
there wasn't any more terror. E specially in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.240
Afghanistan we saw a lot of suicide attacks&nbsp;
against international coalition forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:14.880
and against the civilian population, which has&nbsp;
been and continues to be a major victim of terror.&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:21.520
The situation now, 20 years later, is the&nbsp;
Taliban are back in power more forcefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.960
than ever before, they really control the&nbsp;
entire country now. And from all that we know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:34.160
al-Qaida's core leadership is still in Afghanistan&nbsp;
and Taliban and al-Qaida haven't severed ties yet.&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:41.920
The relations with the Taliban is very much&nbsp;
back to the roots of 2001. They pledged a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:47.040
sort of allegiance to the Taliban. They are&nbsp;
fiercely loyal to the Taliban, and we can expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:52.000
the Taliban answering the same way. Taliban&nbsp;
always refused to distance themselves from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.920
al-Qaida. And I'm pretty sure now that they&nbsp;
established control over Afghanistan, they will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:04.000
to a certain degree talk about not supporting&nbsp;
al-Qaida, but they will do so on a factual level.&nbsp;

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.080
I think it behooves the Taliban to prevent&nbsp;
al-Qaida from using Afghanistan as a hub or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.960
a staging ground for transnational terrorist&nbsp;
attacks. The Taliban's waiting 20 years to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:22.160
reclaim power. They’ve finally done it. If&nbsp;
al-Qaida launches attacks from Afghan soil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:26.640
the West is going to do something about it.&nbsp;
You know, something that could end up in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.480
Taliban once again being usurped. And so I think&nbsp;
the Taliban wants to avoid that at all costs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:38.560
The question in my mind becomes if al-Qaida is&nbsp;
determined to regenerate and attack the West. Can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:40.480
the Taliban control them?
Sandra&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:45.680
And let's not forget, al-Qaida is not the only&nbsp;
terror outfit present in Afghanistan. There is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:51.040
also ISIS, the so-called Islamic State.&nbsp;
And if you think of that massive suicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:57.280
attack at Kabul Airport during the evacuation&nbsp;
campaign, that was claimed by the Islamic State.&nbsp;

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:06.080
At least on a moral level, the perceived Taliban&nbsp;
victory in Afghanistan has a very strong impact on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:13.760
jihadist movements worldwide. At least from their&nbsp;
point of view, what they see is that a much less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:23.920
sophisticated group of convinced believers, to use&nbsp;
their words, they succeeded in repelling the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:28.160
advanced military in the world, the United States.
And this is happening at the same time, the West&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:33.920
is pivoting from counterterrorism to great&nbsp;
power competition. And so between that pivot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:39.280
to look at the rise of China, a resurgent&nbsp;
Russia dealing with the COVID 19 pandemic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:43.840
There's just going to be fewer resources dedicated&nbsp;
to counterterrorism right at the time that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:45.120
groups are preparing to surge.
Colin&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:50.560
There are estimates right now that the global&nbsp;
jihad jihadist movement writ large is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:54.160
over two hundred thousand fighters,&nbsp;
which would make it approximately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:59.440
four times the size as it was on 9/11.
The United States and its coalition partners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:03.920
its allies, have done a really good job&nbsp;
at eliminating individual jihadi leaders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:08.720
But these decapitation strikes have&nbsp;
amounted to tactics without a strategy.&nbsp;

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:15.360
I do think depending what happens in Afghanistan,&nbsp;
what happens in Syria and what happens elsewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:24.480
we could see future attacks that look like what we&nbsp;
saw in Paris in November 2015 at the Bataclan. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:29.680
Brussels in March 2016, or you know, vehicle&nbsp;
attacks like we've seen in Nice and Berlin.&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:33.200
The central question is which area of&nbsp;
operations they (AQ) are focusing in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:41.520
I think for the next time in the foreseeable&nbsp;
future, they will focus on the Asian area because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:46.560
they have to reestablish their global outreach,&nbsp;
but they will not stop to plan international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:50.560
attacks against Western targets as well.
We've been trying to drain al-Qaida support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:56.000
for the past 20 years. But every time&nbsp;
we use a drone that kills civilians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:01.120
we're contributing to sympathy and support for&nbsp;
al-Qaida. Now, if you look at the situation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:09.360
Afghanistan at the moment, the US is bereft&nbsp;
of a robust intelligence footprint there.&nbsp;

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:16.640
The war against terror was not successful the&nbsp;
way it was conducted. The war against terror was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:21.120
counterproductive and in the end&nbsp;
led to more terror. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:27.680
it helped the Taliban to return to power.
If ISIS-K continues to be an issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.280
and If the US continues to launch over the&nbsp;
horizon counterterrorism operations, we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:38.160
likely to see more collateral damage and we're&nbsp;
likely to see that collateral damage translate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:43.680
into more support for jihadi groups.
Let's not view everything as just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:50.560
a militaristic problem. Let's not combat&nbsp;
terror with weapons only. It needs a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:58.320
different approach. It needs diplomacy.&nbsp;
It needs development. It needs dialogue.

